{"data":[{"id":"10.5281/zenodo.23487309","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.5281/zenodo.23487309","identifiers":[],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tao","familyName":"Zeng","name":"Zeng, Tao","nameIdentifiers":[],"affiliation":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhili","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Zhili","nameIdentifiers":[],"affiliation":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Multidimensional leaf-stem trait coordination and its selective scale dependence in woody plants"}],"publisher":"Zenodo","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-10-20","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"Dataset","resourceTypeGeneral":"Dataset","citeproc":"dataset","bibtex":"misc","ris":"DATA","resourceType":""},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"HasVersion","relatedIdentifier":"10.5281/zenodo.23487310","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":null,"rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Coordination between leaf and stem traits reflects trade-offs among resource acquisition, structural support, and water transport, providing insights into plant functional strategies. However, whether leaf-stem trait coordination is consistent across biological scales and how functional types and climate jointly shape these relationships remain unclear. We examined eight leaf and stem traits measured on current-year twigs and their attached leaves in 562 individuals representing 99 woody angiosperm species across five forest sites in eastern China. We assessed functional-type differentiation, interspecific and intraspecific variation, and scale-dependent trait coordination. Leaf phenology and mycorrhizal type were associated with distinct subsets of traits, with stronger multivariate differentiation between deciduous and evergreen species than between arbuscular mycorrhizal and ectomycorrhizal species. Leaf traits exhibited predominantly interspecific variation, whereas several stem traits retained substantial intraspecific variation. Despite these differences, most trait relationships maintained consistent directions without significant differences between within-species and between-species slopes. However, the leaf area-leaf area loading relationship exhibited significant scale dependence, while associations involving stem mass fraction and Huber value varied among functional types. Principal component analysis revealed multiple dimensions of trait variation rather than a single dominant economic axis, with climatic conditions and functional types showing selective associations with different dimensions. These findings demonstrate that leaf-stem trait coordination combines cross-scale consistency with trait- and functional-type-dependent variation, highlighting the importance of integrating hierarchical variation and multidimensional trait organization to understand woody plant functional strategies."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://zenodo.org/doi/10.5281/zenodo.23487309","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"api","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":1,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-10-20T01:33:05Z","registered":"2026-10-20T01:33:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-20T01:33:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"cern.zenodo","type":"clients"}}}}],"meta":{"total":139607868,"totalPages":10000,"page":1},"links":{"self":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?page%5Bsize%5D=1","next":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?page%5Bnumber%5D=2\u0026page%5Bsize%5D=1"}}